1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,669
You tap your card for a
hundred-dollar pair of shoes,

2
00:00:02,669 --> 00:00:04,038
and four companies get paid.

3
00:00:04,038 --> 00:00:06,820
The shop keeps ninety-seven
dollars and twenty-five cents.

4
00:00:07,947 --> 00:00:08,487
It's noon.

5
00:00:09,187 --> 00:00:11,847
You hold a premium rewards
card, the terminal beeps,

6
00:00:12,307 --> 00:00:14,227
and for a second, nothing
else seems to happen.

7
00:00:16,047 --> 00:00:19,186
Behind that one tap, two
seventy-five leaves the sale.

8
00:00:19,186 --> 00:00:23,298
Two dollars twenty heads to your bank,
and sixteen cents heads to Visa.

9
00:00:23,298 --> 00:00:27,407
The last thirty-nine cents goes to the
company running the card machine

10
00:00:27,407 --> 00:00:28,606
and the shop's bank.

11
00:00:29,767 --> 00:00:32,207
Here is the trip those
coins take on one map.

12
00:00:32,787 --> 00:00:34,507
Your money starts here at your bank.

13
00:00:35,647 --> 00:00:37,667
It ends over there, at the shop's bank.

14
00:00:38,567 --> 00:00:40,167
Card money doesn't drop into the till.

15
00:00:41,087 --> 00:00:44,747
It reaches the shop's account later,
often a day or more after the tap.

16
00:00:45,867 --> 00:00:49,673
Picture your payment as a cart carrying
one hundred coins down a toll road

17
00:00:49,673 --> 00:00:50,941
between those two banks.

18
00:00:50,941 --> 00:00:52,767
Each gate on that road keeps a few.

19
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:56,882
The company that runs the road
is the card network,

20
00:00:56,882 --> 00:00:58,488
such as Visa or Mastercard.

21
00:00:58,488 --> 00:01:02,731
Together those two carry more than eighty
percent of American credit card

22
00:01:02,731 --> 00:01:03,300
spending.

23
00:01:04,453 --> 00:01:08,113
The network sets the toll prices and
passes every message between the banks.

24
00:01:08,913 --> 00:01:11,213
Your coins stay in the hands of
the banks at each end.

25
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:14,905
In a normal sale, the tolls
come out of the cart,

26
00:01:14,905 --> 00:01:17,407
so your statement still says
a hundred dollars.

27
00:01:17,407 --> 00:01:19,438
The shop is the one who receives less.

28
00:01:20,567 --> 00:01:23,687
The shop agreed to that deal the day it
put a card sticker on its door.

29
00:01:24,747 --> 00:01:26,687
One more stop sits right next to the shop.

30
00:01:27,347 --> 00:01:30,387
This business runs the card machine and
talks to the road for the shop.

31
00:01:31,087 --> 00:01:32,327
Call it the terminal company.

32
00:01:34,233 --> 00:01:36,293
So four hands wait for
coins from your cart.

33
00:01:37,173 --> 00:01:39,273
Many people would guess that
Visa grabs the most.

34
00:01:39,953 --> 00:01:42,553
Of the slices we can price,
Visa's is the smallest,

35
00:01:42,873 --> 00:01:44,293
and your own bank takes the biggest.

36
00:01:45,367 --> 00:01:47,789
The first coins never leave
your side of town.

37
00:01:47,789 --> 00:01:51,810
When your bank pays toward the shop, it
sends ninety-seven dollars and eighty

38
00:01:51,810 --> 00:01:52,171
cents.

39
00:01:52,171 --> 00:01:53,867
Two dollars twenty stays behind.

40
00:01:55,020 --> 00:01:59,700
That cut is called interchange: a fee the
shop's side pays your bank whenever

41
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:00,780
its card is used.

42
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:04,200
On a sale like this one, it is
by far the largest slice.

43
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:06,293
The price is public.

44
00:02:06,853 --> 00:02:09,793
Visa prints it in a long
table, sorted by card type,

45
00:02:10,193 --> 00:02:13,673
by kind of shop, and by whether the
card was tapped or typed in.

46
00:02:14,713 --> 00:02:19,127
At the standard store rate, a basic
credit card costs one point five one

47
00:02:19,127 --> 00:02:20,577
percent plus ten cents.

48
00:02:20,577 --> 00:02:23,173
On your shoes, that is a dollar sixty-one.

49
00:02:24,333 --> 00:02:29,156
Swap in your premium rewards card, and the
rate becomes two point one percent

50
00:02:29,156 --> 00:02:30,145
plus ten cents.

51
00:02:30,145 --> 00:02:34,040
Same shoes, same shop, and now the
toll is two dollars twenty.

52
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:37,008
Some small businesses
qualify for cheaper rows,

53
00:02:37,008 --> 00:02:39,604
but our shoe store pays the standard one.

54
00:02:41,520 --> 00:02:43,480
Why would your bank like
the shop to pay more?

55
00:02:43,980 --> 00:02:46,140
You need to know something
about how your bank earns.

56
00:02:47,233 --> 00:02:49,333
Say you pay your card off
in full every month,

57
00:02:50,193 --> 00:02:52,293
then your bank collects no
interest on your shopping.

58
00:02:53,093 --> 00:02:57,193
For customers like that, the fee on each
tap is one of the main ways the

59
00:02:57,233 --> 00:02:57,993
bank gets paid.

60
00:02:59,067 --> 00:03:01,347
So banks fight for the top
spot in your wallet,

61
00:03:01,867 --> 00:03:03,687
and that same fee helps pay for the fight.

62
00:03:04,567 --> 00:03:06,727
Points, miles, cashback.

63
00:03:07,907 --> 00:03:11,767
A premium card promises more of them,
and it carries a higher toll.

64
00:03:12,833 --> 00:03:15,559
Say your card pays one and a
half percent back.

65
00:03:15,559 --> 00:03:19,193
A dollar fifty of that two-twenty
rolls home to you as rewards.

66
00:03:19,193 --> 00:03:21,293
Your bank holds on to seventy cents.

67
00:03:22,433 --> 00:03:23,633
Look where those rewards started.

68
00:03:24,173 --> 00:03:26,133
They came out of the cart that
was headed for the shop.

69
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:29,392
Now the cart reaches the middle gate.

70
00:03:29,392 --> 00:03:33,025
Visa bills the shop's side point
one four percent of the sale,

71
00:03:33,025 --> 00:03:36,255
plus about two cents for
handling the approval request.

72
00:03:36,255 --> 00:03:38,158
Together, that is sixteen cents.

73
00:03:40,013 --> 00:03:43,365
Sixteen cents looks tiny, so
zoom all the way out.

74
00:03:43,365 --> 00:03:48,097
In 2025, Visa and Mastercard cards
issued in America carried almost ten

75
00:03:48,097 --> 00:03:50,133
trillion dollars of purchases.

76
00:03:51,200 --> 00:03:52,840
The last stop is beside the shop.

77
00:03:53,700 --> 00:03:56,300
Many small shops never deal with
each party on this road.

78
00:03:56,920 --> 00:04:00,880
They pay the terminal company one flat
price, and it settles up with everyone

79
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:01,180
else.

80
00:04:02,373 --> 00:04:06,909
Stripe, one such company, lists two
point seven percent plus five cents

81
00:04:06,909 --> 00:04:08,359
for an in-person card.

82
00:04:08,359 --> 00:04:11,573
On your hundred dollars, that
is two seventy-five.

83
00:04:11,573 --> 00:04:13,526
The shop sees one clean price.

84
00:04:14,627 --> 00:04:18,007
Out of that, your bank's two-twenty and
Visa's sixteen cents get paid.

85
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:21,798
The thirty-nine cents left
over is the markup,

86
00:04:21,798 --> 00:04:24,784
the price the shop's side
charges for its own work.

87
00:04:24,784 --> 00:04:27,712
The terminal company and the
shop's bank share it,

88
00:04:27,712 --> 00:04:30,007
and their split isn't on the price tag.

89
00:04:30,007 --> 00:04:33,560
This is the part that shopping
around or bargaining can move.

90
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:36,508
Now swap the basic card back in.

91
00:04:36,508 --> 00:04:40,180
The shop still pays two seventy-five,
but your bank's slice drops.

92
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:43,740
The shop's side keeps ninety-eight
cents instead of thirty-nine.

93
00:04:45,613 --> 00:04:48,883
A flat price can even backfire on
the company charging it.

94
00:04:48,883 --> 00:04:51,929
Sell a five-dollar pair of
socks to your premium card,

95
00:04:51,929 --> 00:04:53,313
and watch the ten cents.

96
00:04:54,433 --> 00:04:58,015
The terminal company charges the
shop eighteen and a half cents.

97
00:04:58,015 --> 00:05:00,881
Your bank's interchange alone
is twenty and a half.

98
00:05:00,881 --> 00:05:05,013
Add Visa's share, and the terminal
company ends up about five cents short.

99
00:05:06,113 --> 00:05:09,876
A flat price is a bet that bigger
sales will cover the small ones.

100
00:05:09,876 --> 00:05:12,797
For the shop, those socks cost
almost four percent,

101
00:05:12,797 --> 00:05:15,713
because the fixed cents bite
harder on a tiny sale.

102
00:05:17,633 --> 00:05:19,200
So your shoe cart arrives.

103
00:05:19,200 --> 00:05:24,020
You were charged one hundred dollars, and the
shop's account receives ninety-seven

104
00:05:24,020 --> 00:05:24,775
twenty-five.

105
00:05:24,775 --> 00:05:29,069
Sell a thousand pairs that way, and the
missing money equals the price of

106
00:05:29,069 --> 00:05:30,230
twenty-seven pairs.

107
00:05:32,100 --> 00:05:32,960
That is the whole trip.

108
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:37,320
Yet someone on this map helps pay for it
without using a credit card at

109
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:37,560
all.

110
00:05:38,647 --> 00:05:42,947
Shops often don't charge less for cash,
so the card toll gets built into

111
00:05:42,987 --> 00:05:43,927
the price on the tag.

112
00:05:45,067 --> 00:05:47,727
The man behind you hands over
cash for the same shoes.

113
00:05:48,207 --> 00:05:49,667
He sends no cart down the road.

114
00:05:50,547 --> 00:05:52,587
He still covers part of the
toll through the price,

115
00:05:52,987 --> 00:05:54,347
and no points come back to him.

116
00:05:55,447 --> 00:05:59,593
In 2010, economists at the Boston Fed
estimated the size of that handover.

117
00:05:59,593 --> 00:06:03,629
On average, a household that skipped
credit cards passed about a hundred

118
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:06,667
and fifty dollars a year to
households that used them.

119
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:10,380
Card spending and rewards tend
to rise with income.

120
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:14,560
So even after counting rewards, the study
found money moving from lower-income

121
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:16,300
households to higher-income ones.

122
00:06:17,347 --> 00:06:19,627
To be fair to the road, the
toll buys something real.

123
00:06:20,587 --> 00:06:23,227
The approval from our last video
was a promise from your bank,

124
00:06:23,767 --> 00:06:25,367
so the shop can sell to a stranger.

125
00:06:26,287 --> 00:06:29,787
The shop also has less cash to count
and less change to run out of.

126
00:06:31,633 --> 00:06:33,393
So how low can a toll like this go?

127
00:06:34,213 --> 00:06:36,413
In Indonesia, part of the answer is zero.

128
00:06:37,567 --> 00:06:41,056
Small shops there often show a
printed square called QRIS.

129
00:06:41,056 --> 00:06:45,367
It is a national QR code standard that
bank apps and e-wallets can scan.

130
00:06:46,467 --> 00:06:49,507
Here, the central bank, Bank
Indonesia, sets the toll.

131
00:06:50,487 --> 00:06:53,887
Under its rules, the shop pays it and
may not add it to your bill.

132
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,788
Since October 2026, a QRIS payment up to a
hundred thousand rupiah has cost

133
00:06:59,788 --> 00:07:01,993
shops no fee, whatever their size.

134
00:07:01,993 --> 00:07:06,214
The tiniest businesses keep that zero
up to five hundred thousand.

135
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:12,042
Above a hundred thousand, a regular
small, medium or large business pays

136
00:07:12,042 --> 00:07:13,702
zero point seven percent.

137
00:07:13,702 --> 00:07:17,340
On a bill of one million rupiah,
that is seven thousand.

138
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:22,422
Put that rate on a hundred-dollar
sale, and you get seventy cents.

139
00:07:22,422 --> 00:07:25,540
On the card road, the shop
gave up two seventy-five.

140
00:07:26,667 --> 00:07:28,067
The comparison isn't perfect.

141
00:07:28,607 --> 00:07:31,047
A toll this low leaves less
room for rich rewards,

142
00:07:31,387 --> 00:07:33,207
and a central bank writes the price list.

143
00:07:35,033 --> 00:07:37,241
So your two seventy-five never vanished.

144
00:07:37,241 --> 00:07:41,441
Two dollars twenty went to your bank, and
some rolled back to you as rewards.

145
00:07:41,441 --> 00:07:45,587
Sixteen cents went to the road's owner,
and thirty-nine stayed on the shop's

146
00:07:45,587 --> 00:07:45,910
side.

147
00:07:47,747 --> 00:07:50,467
And the tag may have carried the
toll before you ever tapped.

148
00:07:51,367 --> 00:07:55,627
So next time a shop offers a cash
discount, look at it as a way around the

149
00:07:55,687 --> 00:07:56,207
toll road.

150
00:07:58,067 --> 00:08:02,026
Next, a short one about a forgotten
wallet, the story that launched a famous

151
00:08:02,087 --> 00:08:02,407
card.

152
00:08:03,307 --> 00:08:05,747
The man who promoted it later
wrote that he invented it.
